## Google sign-in

People sign in with their Google account. The backend does the whole OpenID Connect sign-in and keeps a signed, HttpOnly session cookie. The browser never gets a Google token. Only the emails in `AUTH_ALLOWED_EMAILS` get access. Until every required setting is set, every protected API route returns 503 `auth_not_configured`. The app gives no data to anyone while it is not configured.

### Settings

| Setting | Required | Value |
|---|---|---|
| `GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID` | Yes | Client ID of this app's own Google OAuth client. |
| `GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET` | Yes | Client secret of that client. Keep it on the server only. |
| `SESSION_SECRET` | Yes | At least 32 random characters. Render generates it. Changing it signs everybody out. |
| `AUTH_ALLOWED_EMAILS` | Yes | Comma-separated emails that may sign in. Empty means nobody. |
| `APP_BASE_URL` | {% if deploy_target == "render" %}Local and custom domains{% else %}Yes{% endif %} | The address people open, for example `http://localhost:8000`. On Render it defaults to `RENDER_EXTERNAL_URL`. It must be `https`, except `http` on `localhost` or `127.0.0.1`. |
| `SESSION_MAX_AGE_SECONDS` | No | Session length. Default `28800` (8 hours). |
| `AGENT_CREDENTIALS` | No | Agent key hashes for the CLI and MCP server. See "Agent keys". |

These settings load only from this app's `.env` and the process environment. They never load from the shared `~/.agentic-apps.env`.

{% if deploy_target == "render" %}### Set up on Render

Do these steps in this order. Render assigns the service URL when you apply the Blueprint, and Google needs that exact URL.

1. Apply the Blueprint (see "Deployment"). Leave `GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID`, `GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET`, `AUTH_ALLOWED_EMAILS`, and `AGENT_CREDENTIALS` empty. The app starts and returns 503 on protected routes.
2. In the Render dashboard, copy the service URL, for example `https://{{ app_name }}.onrender.com`.
3. In [Google Cloud Console](https://console.cloud.google.com/), select or create a project.
4. Open **Google Auth Platform > Branding** (on older consoles: **APIs & Services > OAuth consent screen**). Set the app name and the support email.
5. Open **Audience**. Select **External**. Then do one of these:
   - Click **Publish app** to set the status to **In production**. The app asks only for `openid` and `email`, so Google does not require verification.
   - Or keep the status **Testing** and add each person in `AUTH_ALLOWED_EMAILS` as a test user. Google blocks all other accounts with an "access blocked" page.
6. Open **Clients > Create client**. Select **Web application**.
7. Under **Authorized redirect URIs**, add `<service URL>/auth/callback`, for example `https://{{ app_name }}.onrender.com/auth/callback`. Do not add a JavaScript origin. The app sends no request to Google from browser JavaScript.
8. Copy the client ID and the client secret. Google shows the secret only once.
9. In the Render dashboard, open the service, then **Environment**. Set `GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID`, `GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET`, and `AUTH_ALLOWED_EMAILS`. Save. Render restarts the service.
10. Open the service URL and sign in.

If you add a custom domain later, set `APP_BASE_URL` to it and add `<custom domain>/auth/callback` to the Google client.

{% else %}### Set up on Railway

1. Copy the public URL of the app service from Railway.
2. In [Google Cloud Console](https://console.cloud.google.com/), set up the app under **Google Auth Platform**. Publish the app or add your allowed accounts as test users.
3. Create a **Web application** OAuth client. Add `<public URL>/auth/callback` under **Authorized redirect URIs**.
4. In the Railway app service variables, set `APP_BASE_URL` to the public URL. Set `GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID`, `GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET`, and `AUTH_ALLOWED_EMAILS`.
5. Set `SESSION_SECRET` to at least 32 random characters. Keep the secret out of Git and chat.
6. Deploy with these variables. Open the public URL and sign in.

{% endif %}### Set up locally

1. Create a second Google OAuth client (Web application) for local work, so the production secret stays on the app service.
2. Add the redirect URI `http://localhost:8000/auth/callback`.{% if include_frontend %} If you use the Vite dev server, use `http://localhost:5173/auth/callback` and set `APP_BASE_URL=http://localhost:5173`.{% endif %}
3. Put the values in `.env` (see `.env.example`).
4. Open the app at exactly the `APP_BASE_URL` host. If you open `127.0.0.1` while `APP_BASE_URL` says `localhost`, the app first moves you to `localhost`.

### Agent keys (CLI and MCP server)

Agents never use Google. An agent uses an agent key that you create for one person. The key acts only as that person. It can only read, unless you allow writes.

1. Sign in with Google in the browser, then open `/auth/session`. Copy the `user_id` value, for example `google:1234567890`.
2. Create the key. The key goes to a new file with mode 0600. The command prints only the file path and the public entry:

   ```bash
   python -m app.auth.agent_keys new --id laptop-cli --actor google:1234567890 \
       --out laptop-cli.agent-key            # add --write to allow writes
   ```

   Optional: `--expires 2027-01-01T00:00:00Z` (UTC). The key stops at that instant.
3. Add the printed `entry` object to the JSON list in `AGENT_CREDENTIALS` on the server. The list holds only hashes.
4. Give the key file to the agent's machine. Never print the key, paste it into a chat, or commit it. `.gitignore` ignores `*.agent-key`.

To revoke a key, remove its entry from `AGENT_CREDENTIALS`. Render restarts the service. To rotate a key, add a new entry, switch the client to the new key, then remove the old entry.

An agent key has no email, so the allowlist does not apply to it. To end a person's access, do both steps: remove the email from `AUTH_ALLOWED_EMAILS`, and remove every `AGENT_CREDENTIALS` entry whose `actor` is that person. Both changes apply when the service restarts.

A client sends the key as `Authorization: Bearer agk.<id>.<secret>` with the `X-{{ app_name | replace('-', '_') | upper }}-Actor-Sub` header. The generated CLI and MCP server send the key for you. See "Machine credential" in the "CLI and MCP Server" section for their settings.

### What each failure means

| Response | Cause | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| 503 `auth_not_configured` | A required setting is missing or invalid. | Set every required setting in "Settings". |
| Login page: "not on the list" (`not_allowed`) | The Google email is not in `AUTH_ALLOWED_EMAILS`, or it was removed. | Add the email, or sign in with another account. |
| Login page: `email_not_verified` | Google has not verified the account email. | Verify the email with Google. |
| Login page: `login_failed` | The sign-in did not finish or the ID token failed a check. | Try again. Check the redirect URI and the client ID. |
| Google page: "redirect_uri_mismatch" | The redirect URI in Google differs from `<APP_BASE_URL>/auth/callback`. | Fix the URI in Google Cloud Console. |
| Google page: "access blocked" | The consent screen is in Testing and the account is not a test user. | Publish the app, or add the test user. |
| 400 `base_url_host_mismatch` | A proxy changes the `Host` header, so it never matches `APP_BASE_URL`. | Set `APP_BASE_URL` to the address people open. |
| 401 `not_authenticated` / `session_expired` | No session, or the session is older than `SESSION_MAX_AGE_SECONDS`. | Sign in again. |
| 403 `csrf_failed` | A browser write did not send the session's `X-CSRF-Token`. | Send the token from `/auth/session`. |
| 401 `machine_auth_not_configured` | An agent sent a key, but `AGENT_CREDENTIALS` is empty. | Add the key entry. |
| 503 `agent_credentials_invalid` | `AGENT_CREDENTIALS` is not valid JSON, or an entry breaks a rule (duplicate, unknown field, bad value). | Fix the list. Browser sign-in still works. |
| 401 `invalid_agent_credential` | `AGENT_CREDENTIALS` has entries, and the request sent something that is not a valid agent key: a Google or Logto token, an unknown key ID, or a wrong secret. | Use an agent key from the tool. |
| 401 `agent_credential_expired` | The entry's `expires` instant has passed. | Create a new key. |
| 403 `missing_actor_sub_header` / `actor_mismatch` | The actor header is missing, or it names a different person than the key. | Send the key's own actor. |
| 403 `missing_scope` | A read-only key tried to write. | Create a key with `--write`. |

