{% if include_cli and include_backend %}{% set APP_UPPER = app_name.replace('-', '_').upper() %}{% set GOOGLE_MODE = (auth_provider | default('logto')) == 'google' %}## CLI and MCP Server

This app has a CLI (`{{ app_name }}`) and an MCP server (`{{ app_name }}-mcp`). Both use the same typed operations in `app/cli/operations.py`. Both call this app's HTTP API with a machine credential. Neither opens the database, so the API's auth and per-user data scope always apply.

| Operation ID | CLI | MCP tool | Access |
|---|---|---|---|
| `items_list` | `{{ app_name }} items list` | yes | read |
| `items_get` | `{{ app_name }} items get <id>` | yes | read |
| `items_create` | `{{ app_name }} items create --name <name>` | only with writes turned on | write |
| `items_update` | `{{ app_name }} items update <id> --name <name>` | only with writes turned on | write |
| `items_delete` | `{{ app_name }} items delete <id> --yes` | never | destructive |

The operation ID is also the FastAPI `operationId` on the route. `{{ app_name }} agent-context --json` lists every operation with its input and output JSON Schema.

### Rules

- The CLI and the MCP server act for one user, `{{ APP_UPPER }}_USER_SUB`. No command or tool takes a user argument.
- The MCP server offers read tools only. Set `{{ APP_UPPER }}_MCP_ALLOW_WRITES=true` where the server runs to add `items_create` and `items_update`.
- Turn on writes only when the user controls all item text. Text inside returned items can influence the write calls that a model makes.
- The read-only default limits what the MCP host is offered. It is not server-side authorization. Anyone who holds the credential can call the API directly.
{% if GOOGLE_MODE %}- The server enforces the agent key's scope. A read-only key cannot write.
{% else %}- The server enforces one required scope (`read` by default) for every route. Anyone who holds the M2M secret can read and write the allowed users' items.
{% endif %}- `items delete` needs `--yes`. Options that you leave out are not sent, so `items update <id> --name X` keeps the description.
- With `--json`, the CLI prints one JSON object. On failure it prints `{"status": "failed", "error_code": ..., "exit_code": ...}`.
- An MCP tool error reads `Error executing tool <tool>: <error_code>: <message>`.
- Error messages never contain a token, a secret, or an agent key.

### Machine credential

{% if GOOGLE_MODE %}Google sign-in is for people in a browser only. The CLI and the MCP server use an **agent key** that this app's server configures. A Google token never works as a machine credential.

1. Sign in to the app once with Google. Open `/auth/session` and copy `user_id` (for example `google:1234`).
2. Create a key in the app directory. Read-only is the default. Add `--write` only if the agent must create or change items.

   ```bash
   python -m app.auth.agent_keys new --id cli --actor google:1234 --out cli.agent-key
   ```

   The command writes the key to `cli.agent-key` with mode 0600 and prints only the server entry. Do not print or `cat` the key file. `.gitignore` ignores `*.agent-key`.
3. Add the printed entry to the `AGENT_CREDENTIALS` JSON list in the web service's environment. The server stores only the hash of the key.
4. Where the CLI or MCP server runs, set `{{ APP_UPPER }}_API_URL`, `{{ APP_UPPER }}_USER_SUB` (the same `user_id`), and `{{ APP_UPPER }}_AGENT_KEY_FILE` (the key file path). `{{ APP_UPPER }}_AGENT_KEY` (the key itself) is a fallback only.

To revoke a key, remove its entry from `AGENT_CREDENTIALS`. To rotate, add a new entry, switch the client, then remove the old entry. A running MCP server keeps the key it loaded first, so restart it after you switch keys.
{% else %}The CLI and the MCP server use this app's Logto M2M client. Set these where they run: `{{ APP_UPPER }}_API_URL`, `{{ APP_UPPER }}_LOGTO_ENDPOINT`, `{{ APP_UPPER }}_API_RESOURCE`, `{{ APP_UPPER }}_M2M_APP_ID`, `{{ APP_UPPER }}_M2M_APP_SECRET`, and `{{ APP_UPPER }}_USER_SUB`. The server must list that user in `{{ APP_UPPER }}_M2M_ACTOR_SUBS`. See `AGENT_CONTEXT.md` for the identity model.
{% endif %}
A missing or malformed setting makes the CLI exit with code 5 and makes each MCP tool return `misconfigured`, before any network call.

### Add the MCP server to Claude Code

Install the extra, then add the server with local scope so its config stays out of the repository:

```bash
pip install -e '.[mcp]'
claude mcp add-json --scope local {{ app_name }} "$(cat <<'JSON'
{
  "type": "stdio",
  "command": "/absolute/path/to/{{ app_name }}/.venv/bin/{{ app_name }}-mcp",
  "env": {
    "{{ APP_UPPER }}_API_URL": "${{ '{' }}{{ APP_UPPER }}_API_URL}",
    "{{ APP_UPPER }}_USER_SUB": "${{ '{' }}{{ APP_UPPER }}_USER_SUB}",
{% if GOOGLE_MODE %}    "{{ APP_UPPER }}_AGENT_KEY_FILE": "${{ '{' }}{{ APP_UPPER }}_AGENT_KEY_FILE}"
{% else %}    "{{ APP_UPPER }}_LOGTO_ENDPOINT": "${{ '{' }}{{ APP_UPPER }}_LOGTO_ENDPOINT}",
    "{{ APP_UPPER }}_API_RESOURCE": "${{ '{' }}{{ APP_UPPER }}_API_RESOURCE}",
    "{{ APP_UPPER }}_M2M_APP_ID": "${{ '{' }}{{ APP_UPPER }}_M2M_APP_ID}",
    "{{ APP_UPPER }}_M2M_APP_SECRET": "${{ '{' }}{{ APP_UPPER }}_M2M_APP_SECRET}"
{% endif %}  }
}
JSON
)"
```

Each `${VAR}` is a reference. Claude Code fills it from your environment when it starts the server. If a variable is not set, the literal text stays, and the server fails closed with a typed error. Keep the real values in your shell environment or a secret manager. Never put a secret value in `.mcp.json` or any other committed file.

{% endif %}## Per-App Token Isolation

This app inherits the eight per-app isolation invariants from `agentic-core`'s
`docs/SECURITY.md`. They are day-1 architectural constraints, not aspirational
goals — Track C tests in `agentic-core` enforce them mechanically. The labels
below match the canonical spec; grep for "Invariant N" to cross-reference.

{% if auth_provider == 'google' %}- **Invariant 1 — Per-app agent credentials.** Agent keys exist only in this app's `AGENT_CREDENTIALS` (hashes only). Each key acts for one person, and a key from another app never matches.
- **Invariant 2 — Per-app audience.** This app has its own Google OAuth client. The backend accepts an ID token only when its `aud` is exactly this app's `GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID`, and it never accepts a Google token as an API credential.
- **Invariant 3 — Per-app session.** The session cookie is signed with this app's own `SESSION_SECRET` and is bound to this app's host, so it cannot sign in to a sibling app.
{% else %}- **Invariant 1 — Per-app M2M client.** This app has its own Logto Machine-to-Machine application. `LOGTO_M2M_APP_ID` and `LOGTO_M2M_APP_SECRET` are unique to this app and never shared with siblings.
- **Invariant 2 — Per-app API resource.** The API resource indicator is `https://{{ app_name }}.<platform-domain>/api` with its own scope set. Every minted token's `aud` claim must match; tokens from other apps are rejected by the FastAPI middleware.
- **Invariant 3 — Per-app SPA + per-app user.** Human login uses this app's own Logto Single-Page Application and a dedicated `app_{{ app_name | replace('-', '_') }}` user, so a leaked session cookie cannot silent-SSO into other apps.
{% endif %}- **Invariant 4 — No shared admin client at runtime.** The `agentic-core-admin` Management API client lives only in the operator's encrypted keystore. It is never written into this app's `.env`, {% if deploy_target == 'render' %}Render env vars, `render.yaml`{% else %}Railway env vars{% endif %}, `Dockerfile`, or `.bootstrap-state.json`.
- **Invariant 5 — One secret store per app.** {% if deploy_target == 'render' %}Secrets live only in the env vars of this app's own Render service. No Render environment group is shared with another app.{% else %}Secrets live exclusively in this app's own Railway project; no Railway "shared variables" span projects. An agent platform can add a per-project allowlist on top.{% endif %}
{% if auth_provider == 'google' %}- **Invariant 6 — Per-app rotation.** Rotating this app's Google client secret, session secret, or agent keys touches no other app.
- **Invariant 7 — Audit-friendly use.** The auth middleware logs the app slug, the auth method, the user ID, the agent key ID, and the decision for every request. It never logs an email, a token, or a key.
{% else %}- **Invariant 6 — Per-app rotation.** Rotating this app's M2M secret, SPA credentials, or API resource touches no other app. Operators rotate one app at a time without platform-wide downtime.
- **Invariant 7 — Audit-friendly token use.** FastAPI middleware logs the app slug, M2M client ID, and actor sub on every validated request so anomalies (cross-app reuse, off-hours bursts) are detectable downstream.
{% endif %}- **Invariant 8 — No long-lived secrets in source.** The shipped `.gitignore` covers `.env`, `*.keystore.json`, {% if auth_provider == 'google' %}`*.agent-key`, {% endif %}`*.bak*`, `*.last-good`, and `.bootstrap-state.json`. The `.env.example` carries only {% if auth_provider == 'google' %}empty values for secrets and local URL defaults{% else %}`<set-in-host>`{% if deploy_target == 'railway' %} and `<generated-by-scaffold>`{% endif %} placeholders and empty values{% endif %}; an entropy gate test rejects any real secret pasted in by mistake.

Variants without auth (`static-dashboard --no-auth`) skip Invariants 1-3, 6,
and 7 because the surfaces they cover do not exist. Invariants 4, 5, and 8
remain universal: even a static dashboard must not embed admin credentials or
commit secrets.

